\section{Proofs}
\label{app:proofs}

Answers are binary and costs finite (every call has a generation cap and the pipeline at most five rounds), so all expectations exist. Propositions~\ref{prop:threshold}
and~\ref{prop:efficiency} concern the one-decision setting of \S\ref{sec:problem}, with cheap answer
correctness $Y_c$, cost $C_c$ and score $S$. For a policy $\pi : s \mapsto \pi(s) \in [0,1]$ other than $\varnothing$,
$J(\pi) = \E[C_c] + \E[C_{\mathrm{ref}}] - \E[\pi(S)\,c(S)]$ and $R(\pi) = \E[\pi(S)\,g(S)]$ by the
tower property, with $g$ and $c$ as in~(\ref{eq:gain}).

\paragraph{Proposition~\ref{prop:threshold}.} Among policies other than $\varnothing$, minimising
$J$ subject to $R \le \eps$ is maximising $\E[\pi(S)\,c(S)]$ subject to $\E[\pi(S)\,g(S)] \le \eps$
over measurable $\pi$ with values in $[0,1]$: a linear program with one constraint, strictly
feasible because $\pi \equiv 0$ has $R = 0 < \eps$. By Slater's condition there is a multiplier
$\mu^* \ge 0$ such that every solution maximises $\E[\pi(S)\,(c(S) - \mu^* g(S))]$ and satisfies
complementary slackness. If $\mu^* = 0$ the constraint is slack and $\pi \equiv 1$, the threshold
$t = -\infty$, is optimal. Otherwise the maximiser sets $\pi(s) = 1$ where
$g(s)/c(s) < 1/\mu^*$ and $\pi(s) = 0$ where $g(s)/c(s) > 1/\mu^*$, using $c > 0$. By (M) the
first set is an upper interval of scores and the second a lower one, and the set where
$g/c = 1/\mu^*$ is an interval between them. On it any $\pi$ is optimal for the Lagrangian, and
complementary slackness fixes only the total risk $\E[\pi(S)\,g(S)] = \eps$. Stopping on the upper
part $\{S > t'\}$ of that interval, with $t'$ chosen so that the total risk equals $\eps$, is
possible exactly when $u \mapsto \E[g(S)\,\mathbf{1}[S > u]]$ attains $\eps$, which holds when $S$
has no atom at $t'$, in particular when its distribution is continuous; otherwise the policy stops
on the atom with the probability that makes the risk equal $\eps$. The result is a threshold
policy, randomised at most on one atom. Comparing its cost with $J(\varnothing)$ gives the optimum
over all policies.

\climb{} searches a finite grid of deterministic thresholds. With a continuous score, the only gap
to this optimum is the grid's resolution; Appendix~\ref{app:randomised} gives an example with an atom,
where randomising helps.

\paragraph{Proposition~\ref{prop:risk}.} \emph{Validity.} Fix $m$ and suppose $\mathcal{H}_m$ holds,
so the losses $\ell_1, \ell_2, \dots$ of threshold $t_m$ on i.i.d.\ calibration tasks have mean
$\mu > \eps$. Each $\lambda_i$ is a function of $\ell_1, \dots, \ell_{i-1}$ with
$0 \le \lambda_i < 1/(1-\eps)$, so $1 + \lambda_i(\eps - \ell_i) > 0$ since $\ell_i \le 1$, and
$\E[1 + \lambda_i(\eps - \ell_i) \mid \ell_{1:i-1}] = 1 + \lambda_i(\eps - \mu) \le 1$. Hence $W_j$ is a nonnegative supermartingale with $W_0 = 1$, and by Ville's inequality $\Pr[\max_j W_j \ge 1/\alpha] \le \alpha$, i.e.\ $\Pr[p_m \le \alpha] \le \alpha$, at the level $\alpha = \delta/2$ of each family.
\emph{Family-wise error.} Let $m^*$ be the first index at which $\mathcal{H}_m$ is true, which
depends only on the distribution. The procedure rejects $\mathcal{H}_m$ only after rejecting every
earlier hypothesis, so any false rejection requires rejecting $\mathcal{H}_{m^*}$, which has probability at most $\alpha$. \emph{Risk.} On the complementary event every threshold in
$\mathcal{C}$ has $\E[\ell] \le \eps$, and $\varnothing$ has $\ell \equiv 0$, so $R \le \eps$ for every member of $\mathcal{C}$. By a union bound over the two families, this holds for every member of $\mathcal{C}_0 \cup \mathcal{C}_1$ with probability at least $1 - \delta$, however the deployed one is selected. \emph{Cost.} The deployed
policy minimises mean calibration cost over $\mathcal{C} \ni \varnothing$.

The i.i.d.\ assumption is used in the conditional expectation above: each calibration loss must be
a fresh draw from the deployment distribution. If the losses share a component that varies from
one run of the model to the next, the bound holds for the recorded run but not necessarily for a
new one.

\paragraph{Proposition~\ref{prop:efficiency}.} \emph{(i)} Let $q(t) = \Pr[S > t]$ be the share of
tasks threshold $t$ stops, and write the risk as a function of it,
$r = \int_0^{q} \tilde g(v)\,dv$, where $\tilde g(v) = g(F^{-1}(1 - v))$ is the gain of the task at
upper quantile $v$ of the score distribution $F$ (with atoms, $r$ is the piecewise-linear
interpolation of the same sums). Moving to a lower threshold raises $q$ and adds tasks of lower
score, whose gain is at least as large by (M), so $\tilde g$ is non-decreasing and $r$ is convex in
$q$, with $r(0) = 0 \le \eps$. The set $\{q : r(q) \le \eps\}$ is therefore an interval
$[0, q_\eps]$, and since $q$ increases along the candidate sequence, the thresholds with
$r \le \eps$ are exactly its first $m_\eps$ members. \emph{(ii)}
$J(t) = \E[C_c] + \E[C_{\mathrm{ref}}\,\mathbf{1}[S \le t]]$ does not increase as $t$ decreases, since
$C_{\mathrm{ref}} \ge 0$. \emph{(iii)} Fix $\eta > 0$ and let $m' = m_{\eps - \eta}$. For $m \le m'$
the losses of $t_m$ have mean at most $\eps - \eta$. The running variance estimate converges, so
the bets $\lambda_i$ are eventually of order $n^{-1/2}$ and bounded so that
$\lambda_i(\eps - \ell_i) \ge -\tfrac12$; with $\log(1 + x) \ge x - x^2$ for $x \ge -\tfrac12$,
$\log W_n \ge \sum_i \lambda_i(\eps - \ell_i) - \sum_i \lambda_i^2(\eps - \ell_i)^2$, whose first sum
grows as $\eta\sqrt{n}$ up to a constant and whose second stays bounded. Hence $p_m \to 0$ in
probability for each of the finitely many $m \le m'$, and with probability tending to one all of
$t_1, \dots, t_{m'}$ are certified. On that event the deployed threshold has mean calibration cost at
most that of $t_{m'}$, and since mean calibration costs converge to $J$ uniformly over the finite
grid, its expected cost is at most $J(t_{m'}) + o_P(1)$. Letting $\eta \to 0$ on a refining grid,
$J(t_{m_{\eps - \eta}})$ tends to the cost of the best threshold at tolerance $\eps$ when $r$ is
continuous, which by Proposition~\ref{prop:threshold} is the optimal cost.

\section{Experimental Details}
\label{app:details}

\paragraph{Benchmarks and splits.} Table~\ref{tab:datasets} lists the benchmarks. MATH, FRAMES and MuSiQue were split once at random (seed 42) into a
development split (20\%), used while building the pipeline and its prompts, and a test split (80\%), used
for every reported number. For BBEH \citep{kazemi2025bbeh} we draw 38 problems from each of its 23 tasks
with a seed fixed per task and use all 874; nothing was tuned on them.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrr@{}}
\toprule
Benchmark & Split & Pool & Used \\
\midrule
MATH-500 & test & 500 & 400 \\
FRAMES & test & 824 & 660 \\
MuSiQue & validation, answerable & 2{,}417 & 1{,}934 \\
BBEH & 23 tasks, 38 each & 4{,}520 & 874 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Benchmarks. FRAMES and MuSiQue are open-book (documents in every prompt).}
\label{tab:datasets}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Documents.} MuSiQue's twenty paragraphs, supporting and distracting (median 2.4k tokens), are given in the dataset's
order, each under its title. For FRAMES we take the two to nineteen articles (median 17k tokens) in each question's \texttt{wiki\_links} (a few entries join several URLs
with commas or are percent-encoded twice; we split and decode them), fetch their current wikitext from the
Wikipedia API in October 2026, and render it to text: infoboxes become ``field: value'' lines, tables one
row per line with cells separated by vertical bars, and common date, unit and list templates are expanded;
references, citations, files and the reference sections are dropped. Public plain-text dumps of these
articles omit tables, which some FRAMES questions need. When a question's articles exceed 80k tokens, each
article keeps its head up to an equal share of that limit, short articles staying whole (6 of 660
questions); 3 of the linked articles no longer exist. The prompt
states that the documents may help answer the question that follows them.

\paragraph{Scoring.} \emph{MATH} answers are compared with the reference by symbolic equivalence
(math-verify), with both sides parsed inside math delimiters and common plain-text notations (fractions
written $a/b$, degrees, Unicode roots and subscripts, single assignments such as $x = 2$) normalised
first; Unicode narrow and no-break spaces and minus signs are mapped to ASCII before parsing. The answer is taken from the last
\texttt{ANSWER:} line or boxed expression of the stored reply.
\emph{BBEH} is scored during the run by the fuzzy match of its official evaluation: prediction
and target are lower-cased, the space after commas and markdown bold are dropped, the first line is kept
and a trailing period removed; the answer is correct if it equals the target, gives the target's option
letter with or without parentheses, equals it numerically, equals it without quotes, or is its bracketed
form. \emph{FRAMES and MuSiQue} are scored by an LLM
judge, since exact match marks correct but verbose answers wrong: gpt-oss-120b at medium reasoning effort
and temperature 0 receives the question, the reference (with MuSiQue aliases) and the extracted answer,
and replies CORRECT only if the answer gives the same entity, number, date or range; partial, hedged or
different values are INCORRECT. Answers already correct under exact match are accepted without a call. We
validated the judge on 60 answers correct under exact match (all 60 judged correct) and by judging a sample
twice (99.5\% agreement). We use medium effort because at low effort the judge was unreliable on degenerate answers.

\paragraph{Models.} gpt-oss-20b and gpt-oss-120b use their released MXFP4 weights, with the reasoning
effort set per request (low at $L_0$, medium at $L_1$ and in the pipeline); Python is declared as a tool on
executor calls. Gemma-4-26B-A4B-it is the released standard checkpoint; at $L_1$ and in the pipeline it is sampled at
temperature 1.0, top-$p$ 0.95 and top-$k$ 64 as its model card recommends, and $L_0$ decodes greedily. Token log-probabilities are returned for the
calls that score $L_0$ and $L_1$. Calls that failed were retried; one of Gemma's FRAMES pipeline runs
exceeded the context window and counts as a pipeline error (1 of 660).

\paragraph{Budget and cost.} There is no per-task budget; a ledger charges every call its prompt and
completion tokens, including hidden reasoning, and the cost of a task, and of each rung in the records, is
read from it, so tool and follow-up calls are included. Each call has a generation cap of 8{,}192 tokens at $L_0$ and of 4{,}096 (gpt-oss on MATH, FRAMES and MuSiQue) or 16{,}384 (all other runs) elsewhere. A reply
that has no answer after its code has run (empty, or code without an answer) receives one closing call
with reasoning at its lowest setting. Pipeline runs that end without an answer (at most 0.5\% of tasks) count as the
pipeline's errors.

\paragraph{Reviewer verdict.} The reviewer is asked to end with \texttt{VERDICT: YES} or \texttt{VERDICT:
NO}. Its verdict is the last such line; failing that, a leading yes or no; failing that, its wording, where
a negative phrase rejects and otherwise a positive one accepts. A review that comes back empty, because its
reasoning filled the generation cap, receives one closing call with reasoning at its lowest setting that
asks for the verdict alone. On the reference runs, reviews
without a verdict line are at most 1.7\% of reviews per setting for gpt-oss (highest on gpt-oss-20b BBEH)
and 0.1\% for Gemma on MATH, but 18\% for Gemma on FRAMES, 10\% on MuSiQue and 35\% on BBEH. On Gemma these
are mostly reviews that fall into repetition, such as a line of dashes, or that end with the reviewer's own
answer to the task instead of a verdict. The wording rule then decides, and it treats a review without an
accepting phrase as a request to revise; reviews whose own final answer equals the proposed answer yet
count as rejections are 0.1\%, 0.3\% and 1.9\% of Gemma's reviews on FRAMES, MuSiQue and BBEH.

\paragraph{Tool.} Python blocks run in a separate process with a 10-second limit; imports of
\texttt{os}, \texttt{sys} and \texttt{subprocess}, file access and \texttt{eval}/\texttt{exec} are
rejected; output is truncated to 1{,}000 characters. A call may execute code up to three times before the
closing call.

\section{Implementation of \climb{} and the Replay Protocol}
\label{app:impl}

\input{sections/_alg_climb}

\paragraph{Betting test.} For a candidate threshold the signed losses $\ell_1, \dots, \ell_n$ on the
calibration tasks are processed in a random order. With $s_1 = \sum_{i<j}\ell_i$ and
$s_2 = \sum_{i<j}\ell_i^2$ over the earlier tasks, the bet on task $j$ is
$\lambda_j = \min\!\big(\tfrac{1}{2(1-\eps)},\ \sqrt{2\log(1/\delta) / (n\,\hat\sigma^2_j)}\big)$ with
$\hat\sigma^2_j = \max\big((s_2 + 0.25)/j - (s_1/j)^2,\ 10^{-3}\big)$, a variance estimate from earlier
tasks shrunk towards $0.25$. The p-value is $1/\max_j W_j$.

\paragraph{Grid and selection.} \climb{} certifies two one-decision families, each at level $\delta/2$.
The first stops at $L_0$ when $S_0 > t$; the second runs both rungs and stops at $L_1$ when
$S_1 = 10\cdot\mathbf{1}[a_0 = a_1] + \bar s_1 > t$. Candidates are $\varnothing$ (always run the pipeline),
then thresholds from conservative to aggressive: for $S_0$, $-0.005, -0.010, \dots, -0.600$, then
$-0.65, -0.70, \dots, -3.0$, then $-\infty$; for $S_1$, the same grid shifted by 10, which stops agreeing
tasks first, followed by the unshifted grid. Each family is tested in order with the betting test, and among
the certified candidates of both families the one with the lowest mean calibration cost is deployed. A rung
without log-probabilities does not stop. For agreement, the text after the last answer line (matched case-insensitively, with or without
markdown: \texttt{ANSWER:}, \texttt{Answer:}, \texttt{**Answer:**}) is normalised as in SQuAD (lower case, no punctuation or articles, collapsed
whitespace) with Unicode spaces mapped to ASCII. Calibration
takes under a second on 1{,}000 tasks.

\paragraph{Evaluation.} Records and replay are as in \S\ref{sec:setup}. Each reported number averages 200
random calibration/evaluation splits (seed fixed per analysis), except the tolerance sweep and the policy
comparisons (100 splits).

\paragraph{Richer policies.} The policies compared in Appendix~\ref{sec:variants} use a training split (20\%), are
certified with the same betting test on a calibration split (40\%) and evaluated on the rest. (A) is the
first family alone and (B) a shared threshold on the two rungs' mean log-probabilities, stopping at $L_0$
when $S_0 > t$ and otherwise at $L_1$ when its mean log-probability exceeds $t$; (C) is the second family
alone and (D) the deployed choice between (A) and (C) at $\delta/2$ each. (E) ranks tasks by a learned
estimate of the signed gain of escalating instead of stopping at $L_1$, $P(\text{rescue}) -
P(\text{harm})$, from a gradient-boosted classifier fitted on the training split to the two scores, their
agreement and the two rungs' costs; its thresholds are quantiles of the training scores, fixed before
calibration.


\section{Checking the Guarantee under i.i.d.\ Calibration}
\label{app:checks}

The violation rates of the main experiments evaluate the selected policy on the recorded pool from which
its calibration half was drawn without replacement, a finite-pool diagnostic rather than the event bounded
by Proposition~\ref{prop:risk}. To check the proposition itself we take the task distribution
$\mathcal{D}$ to be the recorded pool of each setting, uniform over its tasks. We draw a
calibration set of $n = \lfloor N/2 \rfloor$ tasks i.i.d.\ from $\mathcal{D}$ (with replacement),
calibrate \climb{} as deployed, and compute the risk $R(\hat\pi)$ of the selected policy under
$\mathcal{D}$ exactly, as its mean loss over the pool; this involves no evaluation sample. Repeating
5{,}000 times estimates $\Pr(R(\hat\pi) > \eps)$, which the proposition bounds by
$\delta = 10\%$. Table~\ref{tab:iid} reports it with Clopper--Pearson intervals. The largest
estimate is 3.40\% (gpt-oss-20b MuSiQue, $\eps = 2\%$), and every upper 95\% limit is at most 3.9\%.
The failure probability is highest where the cheapest certified threshold sits close to the boundary
$R = \eps$, and zero where the certified family stops well inside it: at $\eps = 10\%$ it is zero on every setting except BBEH, where it is at most 2.4\%.

\input{sections/tab_iid}

\section{Additional Diagnostics}
\label{app:results}

\input{sections/tab_calib_full}

\subsection{Monotone-gain test}
\label{app:monotone}

\input{sections/fig_monotone}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/figure_criterion.pdf}
\caption{Share of tasks the pipeline rescues (up) and harms (down) by decile of $L_0$'s confidence, pooled
over the nine settings where that confidence ranks answers correctly; the net gain falls with confidence,
so stopping pays above one threshold $t^\star$.}
\label{fig:criterion}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:monotone} shows, per setting, the gain of escalating instead of stopping at $L_0$ by
quintile of $L_0$'s confidence, the quantity of condition (M). It falls with confidence on eight of the
twelve settings (Spearman $\rho$ from $-0.3$ to $-1.0$), is flat on gpt-oss-20b BBEH ($\rho = 0.0$) and rises
on Gemma FRAMES, MuSiQue and BBEH ($\rho = 0.87$, $0.70$ and $0.70$). There $L_0$'s confidence is inverted
(AUROC 0.30, 0.34 and 0.22): greedy decoding without thinking loops into repetitions that receive a high
log-probability. Pooled over all twelve settings, rather than the nine of Figure~\ref{fig:criterion}, the gain is therefore U-shaped, from 14.2 points for the least
confident tenth of tasks down to 4.8 and up to 16.9 for the most confident, the last driven by Gemma. The guarantee does
not depend on the condition; where it fails, the threshold on $S_0$ certifies little and the agreement
family carries the savings.

\subsection{Sensitivity and distribution shift}
\label{app:sens}

\input{sections/tab_diagnostics}

Table~\ref{tab:diagnostics} varies the error level and the calibration size, and tests transfer across
benchmarks. A larger $\delta$ buys modest savings, and savings at $\eps = 5\%$ require a few hundred
calibration tasks: with 50 or 100, \climb{} falls back to the pipeline almost everywhere. A policy certified
on one benchmark and deployed on another of the same model loses up to 16.0 points (Gemma, MATH to
BBEH) and more than 5 in ten of 36 cases: the guarantee need not transfer across distributions. At a
tolerance of $\eps = 1\%$, \climb{} certifies nothing on nine settings and spends 98 to 99.8\% of the
pipeline's tokens on the three MuSiQue settings (100 splits), with no violation.

\section{Further Analyses}
\label{app:review}

All analyses in this section replay the per-task records released with the paper; intervals over random
splits reflect split randomness only, not independent re-runs of the benchmarks.

\subsection{Ablations of the score and the reference}
\label{app:ablations}

\input{sections/tab_ablations}

Table~\ref{tab:ablations} changes one part of the deployed procedure at a time and replays it on the splits of
Table~\ref{tab:main}.

\paragraph{The score.} The guarantee holds for every score, including a random one: the largest violation rate is
3\% against $\delta = 10\%$. The score decides how much is saved. At $\eps = 5\%$ log-probability and agreement
together save more than either alone and more than a random score: 62\% of the pipeline's tokens on average,
against 67\% for log-probability only, 79\% for agreement only and 67\% for a random score. Adding agreement matters
most where the direct answer's confidence is inverted: on Gemma's FRAMES and MuSiQue, log-probability alone
certifies almost nothing (100 and 99\%), while the deployed score saves 14 and 31\%. At 10 points, where the
team's net gain is small next to the tolerance, a random score spends about as much on average (33 against 35\%):
slightly less than the deployed score on gpt-oss, by 0 to 8 points, and more on Gemma's FRAMES and MuSiQue, where
the certified policy falls back to the team. A misleading score can thus be worse than none, but what it costs is
savings rather than accuracy.

\paragraph{The reference.} The loss can be measured against any reference. The team's first draft, written by the
executor from the plan before any review, is within 2 points of $L_1$ on ten of twelve settings; measured
against it, \climb{} stays within the tolerance and spends 73\% of its tokens on average at $\eps = 5\%$ and
45\% at 10\%. Against $L_1$ itself, with the ladder $L_0 \to L_1$, it spends 89 and 67\%: the cheaper the
reference and the closer it is to the cheap rungs, the less there is to save.

\subsection{Escalation designs under one guarantee}
\label{sec:variants}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_variants.pdf}
\caption{Tokens at $\eps = 5\%$ for five escalation designs certified at the same $\delta$ (20/40/40 splits):
(A) a threshold on $L_0$'s confidence, (B) one log-probability threshold shared by $L_0$ and $L_1$, (C)
stopping at $L_1$ on its agreement with $L_0$, (D) \climb{}, which chooses between A and C on calibration, and
(E) a learned estimate of the signed gain.}
\label{fig:variants}
\end{figure}

Because \climb{} certifies any ordered family, richer escalation designs can be compared with the
deployed one on equal terms: each is held to the same tolerance, and only its cost differs. Read as an ablation,
A drops the second rung, B drops agreement, and C drops the stop at $L_0$.

\paragraph{Agreement and depth.} The agreement of the two cheap rungs is a strong signal where both answer
in a short form: on tasks where $L_0$ and $L_1$ give the same answer, $L_1$ is right 79 to 100\% of the time
on MATH, FRAMES and MuSiQue, against 34 to 86\% when they disagree; on BBEH, whose answers are long and
varied, the two agree on only 24 to 41\% of tasks, and agreement says little for gpt-oss-20b (26 against 25\%)
but much for Gemma (58 against 17\%). Stopping on it (C) is often the cheapest design where the direct
answer is weak, for instance 43\% of the pipeline's tokens on gpt-oss-20b MuSiQue against 65\% for a
threshold on $L_0$ alone (A), and it still saves where $L_0$'s confidence is inverted (Gemma on FRAMES
and MuSiQue, where A certifies nothing). Where the direct answer is already strong, as for Gemma on
MATH, C pays for $L_1$ on every task and A is far cheaper (36\% against 101\%). Choosing between them on
calibration data (D, \climb{}) avoids both failures and is among the cheapest on average, 68\% of the pipeline's
tokens against 69 to 82\% for A, B and C (Figure~\ref{fig:variants}). A learned estimate of the signed gain
(E) matches it on average; it saves more where it can learn that the confidence is inverted (Gemma FRAMES,
63\% against 87\%) and less elsewhere (gpt-oss-20b FRAMES, 86\% against 70\%), and it needs a training
split.


\subsection{What the team adds, by setting}
\label{app:team}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_review.pdf}
\caption{Share of the executor's correct and incorrect answers that the team's reviewer accepts, on
twelve settings (M, F, Q, E: MATH, FRAMES, MuSiQue, BBEH).}
\label{fig:team}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:team} shows why the team's first-draft errors survive review: on every setting the reviewer
accepts most of the executor's correct answers, but also 23 to 58\% of its incorrect ones, so a wrong
plan-guided draft is often passed through rather than revised.

\subsection{Seeding the team with the cheap answer}
An escalated task runs the reference from scratch. Handing the team $L_1$'s answer and asking it to verify
it first did not help in our check: on gpt-oss-20b MATH the seeded pipeline answers 94.0\% of the tasks against 94.8\%
for the reference, and on the 49 tasks $L_1$ gets wrong, the ones escalation is for, 71.4\% against 73.5\%.
Running the reference from scratch also makes
the escalated outcome equal to the reference's, so the loss comes only from stopped tasks.

\subsection{Randomised policies}
\label{app:randomised}
Proposition~\ref{prop:threshold} allows the constrained optimum to randomise when the score has an atom. In
the two-level example with a single score, a cheap rung that is always wrong at cost 1 and a top rung that is
always right at cumulative cost 2, any deterministic policy with risk at most $\eps = 5\%$ must always
escalate (cost 2), whereas escalating with probability 0.95 costs 1.95. With near-continuous scores, as here, the
gap is at most the grid's resolution, and \climb{} deploys deterministic thresholds.

\subsection{Calibration cost}
\label{app:calibcost}
Calibration requires running every calibration task through both cheap rungs and the reference.
Table~\ref{tab:r5} sets that cost against the per-query saving: recording a calibration set costs 1.5M to
79.2M tokens and is repaid after 335 to 2{,}035 deployed queries at $\eps = 10\%$ and 464 to 13{,}297 at 5\%,
the last where \climb{} saves little at that tolerance.

\input{sections/tab_r5}

\subsection{Label quality}
Replies are stored up to 20{,}000 characters, keeping the head and the tail of longer ones, so that the
offline judges always see the final answer; no reply in the final records was cut before its answer.
The remaining source of label error is the
LLM judge on FRAMES and MuSiQue, validated as described in Appendix~\ref{app:details}.

\section{Case Studies}
\label{app:cases}

\input{sections/fig_cases}

Figure~\ref{fig:cases} follows five MATH tasks through \climb{}: a confident early exit, a stop on the
agreement of the two cheap rungs, a rescue by the team, a stop that keeps a correct answer the team would
miss, and an escalation the team also gets wrong.

\section{Prompts}
\label{app:prompts}

\small
\noindent\textbf{Executor system ($L_0$, $L_1$, pipeline).} \texttt{You are an Executor. Answer concisely.
End your reply with a single line: "ANSWER: <final answer>". For ANY computation, put Python code
that prints the result in a \textasciigrave\textasciigrave\textasciigrave python block; you will receive the
output, then give the final answer.}

\noindent\textbf{$L_0$ and $L_1$ user.} \texttt{Question: \{q\} / Final answer.} ($L_1$ differs from $L_0$
only in its reasoning setting and sampling; it does not see $L_0$'s answer.)

\noindent\textbf{Tool follow-up.} \texttt{[Computation result] / \{out\} / Now give the final answer.}

\noindent\textbf{Closing call (lowest reasoning setting).} \texttt{Do not write any more code. Give the final answer now,
ending with the line ANSWER: <answer>.}

\noindent\textbf{Planner.} System: \texttt{You are a Planner. Break down the question into
sub-tasks.} User: \texttt{Question: \{q\} / Provide a step-by-step plan.}

\noindent\textbf{Pipeline executor user.} \texttt{Question: \{q\} / Plan: \{plan\} / Final answer.}

\noindent\textbf{Reviewer.} System: \texttt{You are a Reviewer. Validate the answer.} User:
\texttt{Question: \{q\} / Proposed: \{a\} / Is the proposed answer correct? End your reply with a single
line: VERDICT: YES or VERDICT: NO.} Closing call for an empty review (reasoning at its lowest setting):
\texttt{Question: \{q\} / Proposed: \{a\} / Is the proposed answer correct? Reply with a single line:
VERDICT: YES or VERDICT: NO.}

\noindent\textbf{Revision turn.} \texttt{Reviewer feedback: \{r\} / Revise and give the final answer.}

\noindent\textbf{Judge (FRAMES, MuSiQue).} System: \texttt{You are an impartial grader of short factual
answers.} User: \texttt{Question: \{q\} / Ground-truth answer: \{gold\} / Predicted answer: \{pred\} /
Grade the predicted answer. It is CORRECT only if it gives the same answer as the ground truth: the
same entity, or the same number, date or range (wording, units written differently, and extra
explanation are fine). It is INCORRECT if any specific value differs (a different number, a narrower
or wider range, a year instead of a full date, a different currency or quantity), if it answers only
part of the question, hedges between several answers, or gives no answer. Reply with exactly one
word: CORRECT or INCORRECT.}
